add(NCO,"M","medium","medium","Foreign interest rates rise while the domestic real interest rate is unchanged. What is the likely effect on domestic NCO?","It rises",["It falls","It becomes zero","It must equal national saving"],"Higher foreign returns make foreign assets more attractive to domestic investors, increasing NCO.","trace_foreign_rate_to_nco");
add(NCO,"M","medium","medium","A domestic firm builds a factory abroad. How is the acquisition classified for the home country?","A domestic purchase of a foreign asset",["A foreign purchase of a domestic asset","An import of consumer goods","Domestic residential investment"],"Ownership of productive capital abroad is a foreign asset held by a domestic resident.","classify_foreign_direct_investment");
add(NCO,"M","medium","medium","Foreign confidence in domestic institutions improves. Holding returns fixed, what capital-flow response is most plausible?","Foreign purchases of domestic assets rise, reducing NCO",["Domestic purchases of foreign assets rise, increasing NCO","Foreign purchases of domestic goods fall, raising NCO","NCO remains fixed because risk cannot affect asset demand"],"Lower perceived risk raises foreign demand for domestic assets, increasing the term subtracted from NCO.","trace_risk_to_nco");
add(NCO,"H","hard","hard","Domestic purchases of foreign assets rise by $70 billion, while foreign purchases of domestic assets rise by $95 billion. How does NCO change?","It falls by $25 billion",["It rises by $25 billion","It rises by $165 billion","It falls by $165 billion"],"The change in NCO is +$70 billion − $95 billion = −$25 billion.","calculate_nco_change","calculation");
add(NCO,"H","hard","hard","The domestic real interest rate rises, but political risk also rises sharply. What can be concluded about NCO?","The direction is ambiguous because the two forces push NCO oppositely",["NCO falls because the interest-rate change is listed first","NCO rises because the word sharply supplies an exact magnitude","NCO is unchanged because the two forces have equal size"],"Higher domestic returns tend to lower NCO, while greater domestic risk tends to raise it; relative magnitudes are needed.","analyze_competing_nco_forces");
add(NCO,"H","hard","hard","A student says a capital inflow means domestic residents purchased more foreign assets. What is the correction?","A net inflow means foreign purchases of domestic assets exceeded domestic purchases abroad",["A net inflow means both asset-purchase flows were zero","A net inflow is the same as positive NCO","The statement is correct whenever interest rates rise"],"Negative NCO is generated when the foreign acquisition of domestic assets is the larger flow.","diagnose_inflow_outflow_reversal","diagnosis");
add(NCO,"H","hard","hard","National saving is $520 billion, domestic investment is $610 billion, and domestic residents buy $160 billion of foreign assets. How much do foreigners buy in domestic assets?","$250 billion",["$70 billion","$160 billion","$770 billion"],"NCO = S − I = −$90 billion. Since −90 = 160 − foreign purchases, foreign purchases are $250 billion.","solve_asset_flows_from_saving","calculation");
add(NCO,"H","hard","hard","Foreign investors reduce new purchases of domestic bonds while domestic investors do not change their foreign-asset purchases. What happens to NCO?","It rises",["It falls","It is unchanged","It becomes equal to the bond price"],"A smaller foreign purchase flow reduces the term subtracted from domestic purchases abroad, raising NCO.","trace_portfolio_reallocation");
add(NCO,"H","hard","hard","Why does a higher domestic real interest rate lower NCO rather than raise it in the standard model?","It favors domestic assets over foreign assets for both domestic and foreign investors",["It directly increases imports of financial services","It makes every foreign asset riskless","It converts national saving into government spending"],"The relative-return change keeps more domestic funds at home and attracts more foreign funds.","explain_interest_rate_nco_slope");
add(NCO,"EL","elite","elite","Domestic rates rise 2 percentage points, foreign rates rise 3 points, and domestic political risk falls. What can be inferred about NCO?","Its direction is ambiguous: the relative-rate change raises NCO while lower domestic risk reduces it",["It rises one percentage point because flow quantities equal rate differentials","It falls two percentage points because domestic risk and rates move together","It is unchanged because domestic and foreign rates both increased"],"Foreign returns rise one point relative to domestic returns, tending to raise NCO, while safer domestic assets tend to lower it; magnitudes decide the net effect.","evaluate_risk_adjusted_capital_flows");
add(NCO,"EL","elite","elite","Gross capital outflows rise while NCO falls. Which change can explain both facts?","Gross capital inflows rise by more than gross outflows",["Gross inflows fall by the same amount","Domestic purchases of foreign assets become zero","The trade balance becomes unrelated to NCO"],"NCO is a net measure; it can fall despite larger outflows if foreign purchases of domestic assets rise even more.","distinguish_gross_net_capital_flows");
add(NCO,"L","legendary","legendary","Residents initially buy $220 billion abroad and foreigners buy $260 billion domestically. Later both flows rise 25 percent and 10 percent, respectively. What is the new NCO?","−$11 billion",["$11 billion","−$40 billion","$55 billion"],"New outflows are $275 billion and new inflows are $286 billion, giving NCO = −$11 billion.","calculate_compound_nco","calculation");
add(NCO,"L","legendary","legendary","A higher domestic rate attracts foreign funds, but residents become much more pessimistic about domestic institutions. Which statement is strongest?","The net effect on NCO depends on the relative strength of return and risk responses",["NCO falls because foreign investors respond before residents rebalance","NCO rises because resident transactions receive greater accounting weight","The two changes cancel because NX equals NCO"],"Interest-rate and risk channels work in opposite directions, and the identity does not fix their behavioral magnitudes.","analyze_competing_nco_forces");
add(NCO,"L","legendary","legendary","NCO moves from −$80 billion to $30 billion while domestic foreign-asset purchases rise $40 billion. How must foreign purchases of domestic assets change?","They must fall by $70 billion",["They must rise by $70 billion","They must fall by $110 billion","They must rise by $150 billion"],"NCO rises $110 billion. With domestic foreign purchases up $40 billion, foreign domestic-asset purchases must fall $70 billion to make the net rise $110 billion.","solve_gross_flow_change","calculation");
add(NCO,"L","legendary","legendary","A country receives a large foreign direct-investment inflow while its residents also purchase more foreign securities. Why is the sign of NCO not yet known?","NCO compares the two gross flows, so their magnitudes are required",["Direct investment is excluded from capital flows","Security purchases determine NCO regardless of size","NCO is positive whenever either gross flow rises"],"Both transactions enter NCO with opposite signs; knowing that each increased does not identify the net balance.","distinguish_gross_net_capital_flows");
add(NCO,"L","legendary","legendary","Political instability raises desired foreign-asset purchases, and the domestic real interest rate rises in response. How should the second effect be interpreted?","The higher rate partly restrains the initial increase in NCO",["The higher rate reinforces NCO by making foreign assets more attractive","The rate converts the capital outflow into domestic investment","The rate leaves NCO unchanged because capital flight is permanent"],"Capital flight shifts desired NCO outward, while the higher domestic return produces movement toward domestic assets along the new relationship.","trace_capital_flight_feedback");
add(NCO,"L","legendary","legendary","A country has NCO of $45 billion, but gross purchases of foreign and domestic assets are each several trillion dollars. What does this illustrate?","Small net flows can coexist with large offsetting gross flows",["The NCO calculation excludes foreign purchases","A positive NCO requires small capital markets","Gross flows must each equal $45 billion"],"NCO is the difference between two potentially large gross positions.","distinguish_gross_net_capital_flows");
add(NCO,"B1","easy","boss","A foreign company buys a domestic office building. For the domestic economy, this is:","A capital inflow that lowers NCO",["A capital outflow that raises NCO","An export that raises NCO","Domestic saving that leaves NCO fixed"],"Foreign acquisition of a domestic real asset is a foreign purchase of a domestic asset.","classify_foreign_direct_investment");
add(NCO,"B1","easy","boss","If domestic purchases abroad equal foreign purchases at home, NCO is:","Zero",["Positive","Negative","Equal to investment"],"Equal gross asset-purchase flows produce a zero net difference.","calculate_nco","definition");
add(NCO,"B1","easy","boss","Which change tends to reduce NCO?","A rise in the domestic real interest rate",["A rise in perceived domestic political risk","A rise in foreign asset returns","Residents seeking more foreign diversification"],"Higher domestic returns make home assets more attractive relative to foreign assets.","trace_interest_rate_to_nco");
add(NCO,"B2","medium","boss","Outward asset purchases are $340 billion and inward purchases are $410 billion. What is NCO?","−$70 billion",["$70 billion","$340 billion","$750 billion"],"Subtracting $410 billion from $340 billion gives −$70 billion.","calculate_nco","calculation");
add(NCO,"B2","medium","boss","Foreign demand for domestic bonds rises with domestic purchases abroad unchanged. Which result follows?","NCO decreases",["NCO increases","NCO remains unchanged","Gross outflows increase"],"The foreign-purchase term is subtracted, so a larger inflow reduces NCO.","trace_portfolio_reallocation");
add(NCO,"B2","medium","boss","Why can a net capital inflow help finance domestic investment above national saving?","Foreign purchases of domestic assets provide external financing",["Imports are counted as domestic saving","NCO becomes positive when investment exceeds saving","Domestic investment is subtracted from GDP"],"When I exceeds S, NCO = S − I is negative; the counterpart is net financing from abroad.","connect_capital_inflow_investment");
add(NCO,"B3","hard","boss","A country's NCO is −$120 billion and residents purchase $200 billion of foreign assets. What are foreign purchases of domestic assets?","$320 billion",["$80 billion","$120 billion","$200 billion"],"−120 = 200 − foreign purchases, so foreign purchases equal $320 billion.","solve_gross_asset_flows","calculation");
add(NCO,"B3","hard","boss","Domestic rates rise while foreign risk also rises. What is the likely combined effect on domestic NCO?","Both changes tend to reduce it",["Both changes tend to increase it","The rate raises it while foreign risk lowers it","The effects necessarily cancel"],"Higher domestic returns favor home assets, and riskier foreign assets further discourage purchases abroad.","analyze_reinforcing_nco_forces");
add(NCO,"B3","hard","boss","Residents sell foreign assets and repatriate funds while foreigners keep domestic holdings unchanged. What happens?","Gross outflows and NCO both fall",["Gross inflows and NCO both rise","Gross outflows fall while NCO rises","NCO is unchanged because no foreign investor acted"],"Reduced domestic acquisition or retention of foreign assets lowers the positive side of NCO.","trace_portfolio_reallocation");
add(NCO,"LB","legendary","legendaryBoss","Gross outflows rise from $500 billion to $620 billion while gross inflows rise from $560 billion to $650 billion. How does NCO change?","It rises from −$60 billion to −$30 billion",["It falls from −$60 billion to −$90 billion","It rises from $60 billion to $30 billion","It changes from −$60 billion to $90 billion"],"Initial NCO is 500−560 = −60; final NCO is 620−650 = −30, a $30 billion increase.","calculate_compound_nco","calculation");
add(NCO,"LB","legendary","legendaryBoss","A risk shock raises residents' desired foreign assets by $140 billion and reduces foreign desired domestic assets by $60 billion. Before interest-rate feedback, how does NCO shift?","It rises by $200 billion",["It rises by $80 billion","It falls by $80 billion","It falls by $200 billion"],"The first change adds $140 billion to NCO; the second removes $60 billion from the subtracted inflow, adding another $60 billion.","calculate_capital_flight_shift","calculation");
add(NCO,"LB","legendary","legendaryBoss","Domestic investment rises $90 billion while national saving rises $20 billion. If residents' foreign purchases fall $30 billion, what change in foreign purchases of domestic assets is consistent?","They rise by $40 billion",["They fall by $40 billion","They rise by $100 billion","They fall by $100 billion"],"NCO must fall $70 billion. Its first gross-flow component falls $30 billion, so foreign purchases must rise $40 billion to produce the remaining $40 billion decline.","reconcile_saving_and_gross_flows","calculation");
add(NCO,"R","medium","repairQuestions","A student labels positive NCO a capital inflow. Which correction is accurate?","Positive NCO is a net capital outflow",["Positive NCO is a net capital inflow","The sign of NCO has no interpretation","Positive NCO means foreign purchases dominate"],"Positive NCO means domestic purchases of foreign assets exceed foreign purchases of domestic assets.","repair_inflow_outflow_sign","diagnosis");
add(NCO,"BR","hard","bridgeQuestions","The domestic real interest rate rises and NCO falls. How does this change reach the foreign-exchange market?","A lower NCO reduces the supply of domestic currency",["A lower NCO raises demand for domestic currency","A higher rate makes foreign-exchange supply horizontal","NCO affects only goods markets"],"With fewer net foreign-asset purchases, residents exchange less domestic currency for foreign currency, so FX supply shifts left.","bridge_interest_nco_fx_supply","bridge");

