add(FX,"L","legendary","legendary","Foreign demand for domestic exports rises while a lower domestic real interest rate raises NCO. What can be concluded?","Currency quantity rises, while the exchange-rate direction depends on the relative demand and supply shifts",["The exchange rate rises because the export-demand change is stated first","The exchange rate falls because NCO controls both equilibrium variables","Currency quantity is ambiguous because the rate is fixed"],"Higher NCO shifts supply right, and stronger export demand shifts demand right. Both raise quantity but have opposing effects on the rate.","integrate_trade_and_nco_shifts");
add(FX,"L","legendary","legendary","Refer to the graph. Suppose foreign demand caused the move from D0 to D1. With domestic and foreign price levels fixed, how does the new rate tend to affect net exports?","The appreciation restrains NX, creating movement along the new demand relationship",["The appreciation reinforces exports while leaving imports unchanged","The higher foreign-currency price of a dollar makes domestic exports cheaper","The rate change shifts currency supply right by the same amount"],"With price levels fixed, the nominal appreciation is also a real appreciation. Domestic goods become relatively expensive, offsetting part of the initial export-demand increase.","trace_fx_feedback_to_nx","graph",graph("FX-02.webp"));
add(FX,"L","legendary","legendary","Refer to the graph. If a lower domestic interest rate caused S0 to move to S1, which chain matches the new equilibrium?","NCO rises, currency supply rises, the currency depreciates, and NX rises",["NCO falls, currency supply falls, the currency appreciates, and NX falls","NCO rises, currency demand rises, the currency appreciates, and NX falls","NCO falls, currency supply rises, the currency depreciates, and NX falls"],"The supply increase moves equilibrium from A to B at a lower rate; the depreciation tends to increase net exports along D0.","trace_interest_nco_fx_nx_chain","graph",graph("FX-03.webp"));
